+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/relocating/from-florida/#article","headline":"Moving From Florida to Myrtle Beach","description":"What Florida movers gain in Myrtle Beach: a lower home price, a smaller beach town with a full airport and six hospitals, and honest numbers on insurance and taxes.","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/relocating/from-florida/","author":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/about/#devin-day"},"reviewedBy":{"@type":"Person","name":"Timothy Nash","alternateName":"Tim Nash","jobTitle":"Broker-in-Charge"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"},"datePublished":"2026-08-30","dateModified":"2026-10-27","inLanguage":"en-US","image":"https://chapter3realty.com/og-image.jpg","mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/relocating/from-florida/#webpage"},"about":{"@type":"Thing","name":"Moving From Florida to Myrtle Beach"},"keywords":"moving from Florida to Myrtle Beach, Florida to South Carolina taxes, leaving Florida for South Carolina"}</script>
 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/relocating/from-florida/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Why do people move from Florida to Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Four reasons repeat in the national data and reporting: the insurance renewal, condo assessments under Florida's post-Surfside inspection rules, and day-to-day costs that grew larger than the income-tax savings. Some want to be closer to family up north without giving up a mild winter. Home prices matter too: a typical home here cost about $342,000 in July 2026 against about $378,000 statewide there. It is usually not taxes, and the tax section on this page says so plainly."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a halfback move?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A halfback is a person who moved from the Northeast or Midwest to Florida and then moved partway back north, to the Carolinas, Georgia or Tennessee. The usual reasons are to keep a mild winter and the ocean, pay less to own a home, and live closer to family. In a national count of 2025 moves, South Carolina gained about 5,400 net residents 65 and older, more than any state; more of them came from North Carolina than anywhere else, and Florida was next at nearly 1,900."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Myrtle Beach cheaper than Florida overall?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On average, yes. The price data behind our cost of living page puts the Myrtle Beach area about nine index points below the Florida statewide average. Housing is the widest gap: a typical home costs about $342,000 here against about $378,000 statewide in Florida. Groceries and gas cost less here, and home insurance averages about $4,500 a year against about $6,500 across Florida. One category is higher: state income tax, which Florida does not charge. For most Florida households the house and the insurance outweigh the income tax, and the calculator on this page runs your own numbers."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will I pay state income tax after moving from Florida to South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Florida has none and South Carolina taxes income at 1.99 percent up to $30,000 of taxable income and 5.21 percent past it, after a deduction of up to $30,000 for a married couple that shrinks as income rises. A couple on $120,000 of wages pays about $4,300 a year. Social Security is not taxed, and the deductions at 65 leave a modest pension taxed lightly or not at all."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I keep my Save Our Homes cap or homestead exemption if I leave Florida?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The cap's benefit can transfer to another Florida homestead, but not across the state line. Selling ends it either way: the buyer of your house is reassessed at market value. Your South Carolina house is assessed under South Carolina's rules, including the 4 percent owner-occupied rate and, at 65 and older after a year of residency, a homestead exemption on the first $50,000 of value."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is homeowners insurance cheaper in Myrtle Beach than in Florida?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On average, yes. Priced on the same $300,000 house, a Myrtle Beach policy averages about $4,500 a year against about $6,500 across Florida, about $15,600 in Miami, about $10,400 in Naples and about $6,300 in Tampa. Premiums are set per address, so buy through us and we get you written wind, hail and flood quotes on the exact house before you make an offer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will my car cost more to own in South Carolina than in Florida?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The tax on it, yes. Florida registration is a flat fee set by the car's weight. South Carolina bills every vehicle yearly on its value, roughly $360 to $460 on a $30,000 car plus a $50 road fee. Each vehicle also pays a one-time $250 fee at its first registration here, due within 45 days of the move."}}]}</script>
