-<div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/invest/">Invest</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Condo Investing</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">Condo investing</p><h1 class="detail-h1">Are Myrtle Beach condos<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)">a good investment?</em></h1><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated October 4, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">Whether a Myrtle Beach condo is a good investment depends on the building: rental history, HOA dues, the master insurance policy, reserves and upcoming assessments.</p><div style="margin-top:1.8rem"><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/invest/long-term-rental/">Run a condo through the analyzer</a></div></div></div>
+<div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/invest/">Invest</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Condo Investing</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">Condo investing</p><h1 class="detail-h1">Are Myrtle Beach condos<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)">a good investment?</em></h1><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated October 27, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">Whether a Myrtle Beach condo is a good investment depends on the building: rental history, HOA dues, the master insurance policy, reserves and upcoming assessments.</p><div style="margin-top:1.8rem"><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/invest/long-term-rental/">Run a condo through the analyzer</a></div></div></div>
 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The direct answer</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Are Myrtle Beach condos a good investment?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">They can be excellent and they can be money pits, and the difference is almost never the unit itself. It comes down to three things: the building you buy into, the loan you can get on it, and who manages the rentals. Get those three right and a Myrtle Beach condo can outperform most coastal rental markets on price alone. Oceanfront units here still start under $200,000.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">This page covers the three deciders, plus the fees and taxes that surprise first-time condo investors.</p></div></section>
 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Decider one</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">The building matters more than the unit</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two identical units a block apart can earn different amounts because the buildings differ: rental history, HOA dues, the master insurance policy, reserve savings, and upcoming special assessments. The right building can produce far more revenue than a nearly identical unit in the wrong one. We keep a building-by-building map of operations and fees: the <a href="/invest/condo-buildings/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">oceanfront building directory</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Before any offer, we read the building's bylaws, budget, and reserve study, and we ask for its rental history. A pretty unit in a struggling building is still a bad investment.</p><div style="margin-top:1.4rem;display:flex;gap:.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Ask us to check a building</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></section>
 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Decider two</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Condotel financing limits your lender list</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Many oceanfront buildings are classified as condotels: buildings that operate like hotels, with front desks and nightly rental programs. Normal conventional loans often do not work in them, which means larger down payments and fewer willing lenders. What a furnished unit can write off in its first year is on our <a href="/invest/cost-segregation/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">cost segregation page</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">This is where buyers lose deals, so line up financing before you shop. Our preferred lender, <a href="https://brickwoodmortgage.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">BrickWood Mortgage</a>, works these buildings regularly and can tell you which ones close cleanly before you spend on an inspection. <a href="/invest/strategies/dscr-loans/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">DSCR loans</a>, which qualify on the unit's rent instead of your tax returns, are a common fit. We wrote a full guide to this: <a href="/invest/condotel-financing/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">condotel financing in Myrtle Beach</a>.</p></div></section>
