+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "Article", "@id": "https://chapter3realty.com/invest/cost-segregation/#article", "headline": "Cost segregation on a beach condo, and the recapture it creates", "description": "What a study reclassifies in a furnished beach condo, the 100 percent allowance, whether the passive rules let you use the loss, and what comes back at sale.", "url": "https://chapter3realty.com/invest/cost-segregation/", "author": {"@id": "https://chapter3realty.com/#org"}, "reviewedBy": {"@type": "Person", "name": "Timothy Nash", "alternateName": "Tim Nash", "jobTitle": "Broker-in-Charge"}, "publisher": {"@id": "https://chapter3realty.com/#org"}, "datePublished": "2026-09-04", "dateModified": "2026-10-27", "inLanguage": "en-US", "image": "https://chapter3realty.com/og-image.jpg", "mainEntityOfPage": {"@id": "https://chapter3realty.com/invest/cost-segregation/"}, "about": {"@type": "Thing", "name": "Grand Strand short-term rental regulations"}, "keywords": "cost segregation Myrtle Beach condo, cost segregation study beach condo, depreciation recapture rental sale, bonus depreciation furnished condo, Grand Strand investor tax"}</script>
 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Does cost segregation create new deductions?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. It changes when you take them. The total depreciation over the life of the property is the same with or without a study. More of it lands in year one."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can I use the loss against my salary?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Only if the property is outside the passive rules and you materially participate. A managed unit owned by someone with a full-time job usually cannot. The loss carries forward and offsets rent and other passive income in later years. Whatever is left is released at a taxable sale."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does my purchase qualify for the 100 percent allowance?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The allowance is 100 percent for qualified property acquired after the January 2025 date named at the top of this page. Whether your purchase qualifies depends on the acquisition date and the contract. Your CPA confirms it."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What comes back when I sell?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Depreciation on the reclassified items comes back as ordinary income, up to the amount taken. Depreciation on the building comes back as gain taxed at up to 25 percent. A 1031 exchange defers both."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Is a study useful on an unfurnished long-term rental?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Less often. Most of the value a study finds in a condo is the furniture, appliances and finishes that come with a furnished unit. An unfurnished unit has less to move."}}]}</script>
