+### Where a buyer tax credit was tried before
+The federal credit of 2008 to 2010 paid up to $8,000. A study in the Journal of Finance found it raised home sales by about 490,000, near 10 percent. It raised prices about 1 percent by the study's measure. Much of the extra demand came from buyers who would have bought several years later. The authors judged that its benefit in steadying the market likely exceeded its direct boost to buying.
+Here it did not stop a fall. The Myrtle Beach-area price index dropped about 10 percent in 2009 and kept falling until 2012.
+
+### What it could mean for a $50,000 credit
+If a $50,000 credit passed, the same pattern points to more sales and higher prices in the first years. It is about six times the old credit, and no study we found measured a credit that size. How much prices would rise, then or later, is unknown. Our page on the first-time buyer tax credit shows what the last one did.
+
+### Her other plans, with no direct tie to a home sale
+
+- Health care. Reverse federal Medicaid cuts, expand Medicaid in South Carolina, and restore expired marketplace premium help. Limit insurer profits per enrollee, let regulators reject large premium increases, replace prior authorization, and add a public option.
+
+- Drug prices. Imports from Canada, the $35 insulin cap for every insured patient, patent reform, rules for drug middlemen, and more drug prices that Medicare negotiates.
+
+- Families and work. A child care worker apprenticeship, tax credits for child care businesses, a permanent, larger child tax credit, higher wages and paid family leave.
+
+- Taxes and spending. End tax breaks for the highest earners, the carried interest break and breaks for moving jobs overseas. Keep no tax on tips and Social Security for good. Restore free IRS filing. Vote against debt ceiling increases until a balanced budget passes.
+
+- Energy. Suspend the federal gas tax, restore clean energy tax cuts, and cut red tape for new energy projects.
+
+- Congress. Term limits, and two Senate terms for herself. A balanced budget amendment, a stock trading ban, no pay in shutdowns, no congressional pensions, no earmarks, and stronger fraud checks.
+
+- Other. Universal background checks and safe gun storage, a path to citizenship with border security, a digital bill of rights, phones out of schools, Roe v. Wade protections, a ban on corporate PAC and dark money, and a vote of Congress before military force.
+
+## What are Alan Wilson's plans for property tax and housing?
+Wilson proposes nine things that touch homes or what owners pay. They come from his priorities , his agenda as Fox Carolina reported it , and an interview with WIS . Each is a proposal. A state law needs a majority of both chambers and his signature.
+A change to the 4 and 6 percent tax ratios needs two-thirds of each chamber.
