+A 2020 Lincoln Institute study found that the state's system has shifted the property tax burden from homes people live in to rentals, second homes and business property. The South Carolina Chamber Foundation and South Carolina Realtors chose its case-study counties.
+The study counts a group it calls other residential, typically rentals taxed at 6 percent. In Horry County in 2018, that group was 36.4 percent of the appraised value of the property the county assessor values. It was 47.2 percent of the value the tax is figured on. Act 388 has defenders too, such as nohometax.org.
+No plan names who would make up the lost tax. No study we found shows whether the saving would reach tenants or prices. Our page on what Act 388 changes would mean looks at both sides.
+
