+# Annie Andrews wants a first-time buyer tax credit. What the last one did to home prices, nationally and in Myrtle Beach: a small rise nationally, while Myrtle Beach prices kept falling until 2012.
+By Devin Day , Chief Operating Officer, and Abdulla Hijazi , Chief Marketing Officer · Reviewed by Tim Nash , Broker-in-Charge · Updated November 4, 2026
+The $50,000 credit she backs has not passed. The last federal credit, up to $8,000, was open while Myrtle Beach prices fell.
+
+The short answer
+Annie Andrews backs a $50,000 federal tax cut to help first-time buyers pay their down payment. Her plan names no bill. The closest match, H.R. 3475, a credit equal to the down payment up to $50,000, has had no vote.
+The last federal credit, up to $8,000, ended in 2010. It lifted sales and prices a little nationally. Myrtle Beach prices fell through it and until late 2012.
+If a new credit passes, past examples point to more sales and slightly higher prices at first, then fewer first-time buyers for some years. No study measures a credit of $50,000.
+
