+
+## Is there a first-time home buyer tax credit in 2026?
+No federal one. The last federal first-time buyer credit ended in 2010, and the credits proposed since then have not become law.
+The U.S. Treasury wrote in 2024 that “there is currently no tax credit to support home purchases by first-time homebuyers.” State help is different. South Carolina's housing agency, SC Housing, offers down payment help that buyers here can use now. Our guide to SC Housing's down payment help explains who qualifies. Our agents check which program fits a buyer before the offer goes in.
+
+### What exactly did Annie Andrews say?
+Andrews, whom the AP called the winner of South Carolina's U.S. Senate race on election night, wrote this in her affordability plan:
+“Members of Congress from both parties have proposed a $50,000 tax cut to help first-time homebuyers offset the cost of their down payment. This is a great idea, and I’m excited to work with both Republicans and Democrats to get this one done.”
+Annie Andrews, her affordability plan , page 13
+Her plan names no bill. The bill that best matches her words is most likely H.R. 3475, which a Republican introduced and five Democrats cosponsored. That match is our reading of her plan; her campaign did not name the bill.
+
+## What is the $50,000 tax credit in H.R. 3475?
+H.R. 3475 is a House bill for a federal tax credit equal to a first-time buyer's down payment, up to $50,000. Its full terms, from the bill's summary on congress.gov , are below.
+H.R. 3475, the Bipartisan American Homeownership Opportunity Act of 2025 How much A federal tax credit equal to the buyer's down payment, up to $50,000.
+Who First-time home buyers.
+Income The credit starts to shrink above $150,000 of income for a single filer, $225,000 for a head of household and $300,000 for a married couple filing jointly. The limits rise with inflation after 2025.
+Within five years The five years start at the purchase. If in that time the home is sold, rented out or stops being the buyer's main home, the credit is added back to taxable income. Some exceptions apply.
+Builders A builder credit of up to 15% of construction costs, for a home of 1,200 square feet or less. The home must sell at or below 80% of the area's median home price. The credit is 30% when the buyer is a first-time buyer. State housing agencies hand out the builder credits.
+Where it stands Rep. Brian Fitzpatrick, a Pennsylvania Republican, introduced it on May 17, 2025. Its five cosponsors are Democrats. It was sent to the House Ways and Means Committee and has had no vote.
+
+The credit is a federal income tax credit, so it would apply the same way in Myrtle Beach as in any other state.
+
+### Is it the same as the Merkley $50,000 plan?
+No. Sen. Jeff Merkley's Homeownership Promise Act is a savings match, not a tax credit. The federal government would add five dollars for each dollar a buyer saves, up to the same $50,000 total. It is in the Senate Banking Committee.
+
+## How did the 2008, 2009 and 2010 first-time buyer credits work?
+The federal credit was 10% of the home's price, with a cap that changed with the year. A first-time buyer was anyone who had not owned a main home in the three years before. The IRS questions and answers list the basic rules.
+The federal first-time buyer credit, start to finish After April 8, 2008 The credit opens: 10% of the price, up to $7,500. For 2008 buyers it is an interest-free loan, paid back over 15 years.
+Homes bought in 2009 Up to $8,000, and the buyer keeps it. It is paid back only if the home stops being the buyer's main home within 36 months.
+From Nov. 7, 2009 Owners who had lived in the same main home for years can get up to $6,500 on their next home. The income limits rise.
+Before May 1, 2010 The deadline to sign a contract.
+Before Oct. 1, 2010 The deadline to close. Congress moved it from July 1 in July 2010, for buyers already under contract. The credit ends.
+2010 to 2024 tax returns 2008 buyers pay their credit back, a share on each return, over 15 years. The IRS retired the repayment form after 2024.
+
+Income where the 2008 to 2010 credit shrank to nothing Home bought Single Married, joint
+Through Nov. 6, 2009 $75,000 to $95,000 $150,000 to $170,000
+After Nov. 6, 2009 $125,000 to $145,000 $225,000 to $245,000
+
+### Did buyers have to pay the first-time buyer credit back?
+Buyers from 2008 did. Their credit was an interest-free loan, paid back in small shares over fifteen years of tax returns. The IRS retired the repayment form after the last of those returns, for 2024.
+Buyers from 2009 and 2010 kept the credit, unless the home stopped being their main home within three years.
+
+## Did the last first-time buyer credit raise home prices?
+Yes, a little, and it moved sales earlier. A peer-reviewed study in the Journal of Finance measured the last credit across the whole country.
+490,000 more home sales across the country, a rise of 9.8%.
+$2,400 higher median prices (1.1%) in places where first-time buyers were a larger share of the people, for each typical step up in that share.
+53% higher rate of people moving into owning a home.
+
+The added buyers came “from several years in the future”: many would have bought later and bought sooner instead. They were about three years younger than typical first-time buyers.
+The authors conclude that steadying a falling market likely did more good than the added sales themselves. We found no other peer-reviewed measure of the credit's effect on prices to set beside theirs.
+
+## What did Myrtle Beach home prices do from 2008 to 2012?
+Myrtle Beach area home prices fell every year. The federal price index for the Myrtle Beach-Conway-North Myrtle Beach area dropped about 26% from the end of 2008 to the end of 2012, its low.
+Myrtle Beach area home prices, change since the end of 2008 While the federal credit was open Price index, by quarter
+Prices in the Myrtle Beach-Conway-North Myrtle Beach area fell for most of the time the credit was open, rose a little in late 2010, then kept falling to a low at the end of 2012, about two years after the last closings. Federal Housing Finance Agency all-transactions house price index, by quarter.
+Myrtle Beach area price index at the end of each year End of Index Change in the year
+2008 211.91
+2009 191.40 −9.7%
+2010 181.92 −5.0%
+2011 164.86 −9.4%
+2012 156.16 −5.3%
+2013 158.79 +1.7%
+
+### What did the $8,000 credit mean next to that fall?
+Less than one year of falling prices. At the index's 2009 pace, a home worth $200,000 at the end of 2008 lost about $19,000 of value in a year, more than twice the credit.
+The 2009 credit beside one year of falling Myrtle Beach prices What Dollars
+
+The largest 2009 credit $8,000
+Lost by a $200,000 home in 2009 about $19,000
+
+The loss is the Myrtle Beach area index's 2009 change (−9.7%) applied to a home worth $200,000 at the end of 2008. It is an average pace; each house moved on its own.
+The credit did not stop the local fall. The study covers the whole country, so it does not measure how much deeper the fall here would have been without the credit. Prices here turned up only after 2012, and nothing ties that turn to the credit.
+Our home value guide shows what homes sell for here now.
+
+## What other first-time buyer credits have been tried?
+Washington, D.C. had its own federal credit. President Biden proposed a credit that never passed, and Sen. Jeff Merkley proposed a savings match that is in committee. Here they are beside the federal credits.
+Federal credit, 2008 Ended Most $7,500, or 10% of the price Income Shrinks from $75,000 single, $150,000 joint Paid back Yes, over 15 years
+Federal credit, 2009 and 2010 Ended Most $8,000; $6,500 for long-time owners from Nov. 7, 2009 Income Shrinks from $75,000 single, $150,000 joint; from Nov. 7, 2009, $125,000 and $225,000 Paid back Only if it stops being the main home within 36 months
+Washington, D.C. credit Ended Most $5,000 Income Shrinks from $70,000, or $110,000 joint Ended For homes bought after 2011
+President Biden's budget for fiscal 2025 Never law Most $10,000, or 10% of the price, half in the year of purchase and half the next Income Shrinks from $100,000 to $200,000 Cost Treasury's estimate: $31.0 billion over 10 years
+H.R. 3475, the $50,000 credit No vote Most The down payment, up to $50,000 Income Shrinks from $150,000 single, $225,000 head of household, $300,000 joint Within five years Added back to taxable income if the home is sold, rented or no longer the main home
+Merkley savings match In committee Most $5 for each $1 saved, up to $50,000; a match, not a tax credit Income No income limit Rules Homes at or below the local median price; a counseling course approved by HUD
+
+President Biden's credit was a budget proposal that no law enacted. Treasury's cost estimate shows the scale of a $10,000 credit, not what it would do to prices.
+
+## What would it take for Congress to pass a new first-time buyer credit?
+Both the House and the Senate would have to pass the same bill, and the president would have to sign it. The four steps are below.
+
+- A bill in the new Congress. H.R. 3475 is in the House Ways and Means Committee, which writes tax law. A committee vote comes first.
+
+- A House vote. A simple majority of the House passes it.
+
+- A Senate vote. A regular bill needs 60 senators to end debate before the final vote, under the Senate's cloture rule . A budget reconciliation bill has a time limit on debate, so a simple majority passes it.
+
+- The president signs it. The credit then applies on the dates the law names.
+
+CBS News projects that Democrats will control the Senate in the new Congress, with fewer than 60 seats. A regular bill would then need senators from both parties.
+H.R. 3475 already has both parties on it: a Republican sponsor and Democratic cosponsors. Andrews joins the Senate in January.
+Photo: Architect of the Capitol
+The U.S. Capitol, where the House and the Senate would each have to pass a new credit.
+
+## What could a new credit mean for Grand Strand buyers and sellers?
+Past examples give a direction, not a number. Where a credit was tried before, sales rose while it was open, and part of that rise came from buyers who would have bought years later. If one passes here, the same pattern points the same way.
+What past credits did, and what the same pattern points to on the Grand Strand When Where it was tried before If a credit passes here
+First 1 to 2 years: sales Home sales rose by about 490,000 across the country, or 9.8%, and more people became owners. More first-time buyers shopping for the homes they buy, while the credit is open.
+First 1 to 2 years: prices Median prices rose about 1.1% more in places where first-time buyers were a larger share of the people. In the Myrtle Beach area, prices fell 9.7% in 2009 anyway. From no rise at all, when the wider market is falling, to a small rise in places with many first-time buyers.
+5 years and more The added buyers came from several years in the future, and the effect did not reverse at once. Local prices hit their low about two years after the credit ended. Fewer first-time buyers than usual for some years after it ends, spread out, not all at once. In 2009 the wider market moved local prices more than the credit did.
+Size The old credit was at most $8,000. $50,000 is about six times as large. No study measures a credit that size, so the old results are a direction, not a number.
+
+The Myrtle Beach index is up 0.8% from a year earlier in its latest quarter, about flat.
+
+### If you are buying your first home
+A bill can change before it passes, so its amount, income limits and start date are not final. The down payment help from SC Housing is open now.
+Example: Maria rents in Conway and wants to buy her first home this spring.
+She reads about a large federal buyer credit and thinks about waiting for it.
+An agent at Chapter3 shows her that the bill has had no vote in Congress.
+Her Chapter3 agent also finds state down payment help that Maria can use now.
+Maria starts her home search with the state help.
+What this means for you: plan with the help that exists today.
+
+### If you are selling a home first-time buyers buy
+A credit would bring more first-time buyers while it lasts. The last one shows its limit: it did not keep local prices from falling.
+Buying your first home before any credit passes?
