+
+## What else does Annie Andrews plan for housing?
+Three more plans in her affordability plan touch housing: a lower cap on large investors, limits on Wall Street buyers, and tax cuts for homebuilders.
+
+- A lower cap on large investors. The ROAD to Housing Act bars an investor that controls 350 or more single-family homes from buying more, starting in January 2027. Her plan: “We should cut that threshold down several times to 50 homes.”
+
+- No Fannie Mae or Freddie Mac loans for Wall Street buyers. Her words: “I’ll work to ban these Wall Street interests from using Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac mortgages to buy up housing.”
+
+- More homes built. Her plan: “We should provide tax cuts to homebuilders to incentivize more construction,” with more workforce housing and housing near the places where jobs are.
+
+The ROAD to Housing Act became law in July without the president's signature. Its investor ban ends 15 years after it starts. Our guide to what the election means for Myrtle Beach buyers and sellers covers both winners' full plans.
+State property taxes on rentals are changed by the legislature in Columbia, not by Congress. Our page on Alan Wilson's plan to cut the tax on rentals covers that. For the rest of a first purchase here, our first-time home buyer guide goes step by step.
+
