+
+## First-time buyer tax credit questions
+
+### Is there a first-time home buyer tax credit in 2026?
+No federal one. The last federal credit ended in 2010. The $50,000 credit Annie Andrews backs is most likely H.R. 3475, a bill that has had no vote. South Carolina buyers can still use down payment help from SC Housing, the state housing agency.
+
+### Do you have to pay back the first-time homebuyer tax credit?
+Only buyers from 2008 had to, in small shares over fifteen years of tax returns, ending with the 2024 return. Later buyers owed it back only if they sold the home or stopped living in it within three years.
+
+### When was the first-time homebuyer tax credit?
+The federal credit covered homes bought from April 2008 to September 2010. The last buyers had to sign a contract by the end of April 2010 and close by the end of September. Washington, D.C. had a separate credit that ended for homes bought after 2011.
+
+### Is the Merkley $50,000 plan the same as H.R. 3475?
+No. H.R. 3475 is a tax credit equal to the down payment, up to $50,000. Sen. Jeff Merkley's plan is a savings match, not a tax credit. The government adds five dollars for each dollar a buyer saves, up to the same total. It is for homes at or below the local median price.
+
+### Did the first-time buyer credit stop Myrtle Beach prices from falling?
+No. The Myrtle Beach area price index fell about 26% from the end of 2008 to the end of 2012, through the credit and after it ended.
