+
+## What happens to flood insurance in Myrtle Beach on Dec. 11?
+Federal flood insurance expires on Dec. 11 unless Congress extends it. FEMA runs it as the National Flood Insurance Program, or NFIP.
+
+- No new policies FEMA cannot sell a new federal flood policy until Congress acts.
+
+- Current policies stay in force A policy you already have covers you to the end of its one-year term.
+
+- Claims are still paid FEMA pays claims from the premiums it collects. Its borrowing limit drops from about $30 billion to $1 billion.
+
+The flood insurance deadline comes before the new senator
+- Sept. 2 The stopgap spending law is signed. It keeps federal flood insurance running to Dec. 11.
+
+- Nov. 3 Election Day. The Associated Press calls the Senate race for Annie Andrews.
+
+- Dec. 11 Federal flood insurance expires unless Congress extends it. The Congress in office now votes.
+
+- Jan. 3 The new Senate term starts. Sen. Darline Graham's term ends.
+
+- After Jan. 3 Andrews votes on any later extension or long-term flood bill.
+
+### Who votes on the Dec. 11 deadline?
+The Congress in office now. Sen. Darline Graham, appointed in July after her brother, Sen. Lindsey Graham, died, holds the seat until her term ends on Jan. 3. Russell Fry, who represents Horry County in the U.S. House, won re-election and votes before and after January.
+Our guide to what the election means for Myrtle Beach buyers and sellers covers every race and what each office controls.
+
+## Has flood insurance lapsed before, and did closings stop?
+Yes. Congress has let federal flood insurance lapse nine times since 2010. Each lapse ended when Congress passed an extension.
+How long each lapse of federal flood insurance lasted, by the day it started. Chart: Chapter3 Realty, from the Congressional Research Service's record of each lapse.
+
+### What did a lapse do to home closings?
+In the month-long lapse of June 2010, an estimated 1,400 home closings a day were canceled or delayed across the country. During the six-week lapse of October and November 2025, some lenders suspended the flood insurance requirement for buyers.
+A lapse matters most to a buyer with a loan on a home in a high-risk flood zone. The lender must require flood insurance there before closing. Most buyers meet that rule with a federal policy.
+
+## How many Horry County homes have federal flood insurance?
+About 1 in 7 residential buildings in Horry County has a federal flood policy, 20,388 policies in all. Of those, 5,861 are on homes in the high-risk flood zone, where a lender requires one. A home outside that zone can still flood, and our agents check a house's flood zone and flood history before you offer.
+Share of homes with a federal flood policy, three coastal counties County, policies Share of homes
+
+Charleston County · 46,856 policies 31.7%
+Georgetown County · 5,655 policies 20.7%
+Horry County · 20,388 policies 14.6%
+
+Federal flood policies on homes, as a share of all residential buildings in each county. FEMA counts policies, and one policy can cover a whole condo building.
+Map: NASA
+Rain from Hurricane Florence, Sept. 13 to 16, 2018, estimated by NASA satellites. Red marks the most rain. Horry County is the South Carolina coast just south of the state line. The dotted line is the storm's track.
+
+## How much can flood insurance go up each year in Myrtle Beach?
+Under federal law, most flood premiums can rise no more than 18% a year. A second home still on an older discounted rate is different. Its premium must rise 25% a year until it reaches the full-risk price.
+Years Most homes Older discounted second home
+1 +18% +25%
+2 +39% +56%
+3 +64% +95%
+5 +129% +205%
+
+At the lower limit, a premium can grow to more than twice its starting amount in five years. At the higher one, it can reach three times.
+These limits stay the same whoever holds the Senate seat. Only a new law from Congress changes them. Our guide to coastal insurance in Myrtle Beach gives what flood, wind and homeowners policies cost here.
+
+## What can one U.S. senator change about flood insurance?
+A U.S. senator can vote, sponsor bills and serve on committees. One senator cannot change flood insurance alone.
+
+- 1 of 100 is Andrews' share of the Senate's votes.
+
+- 60 votes are needed to end debate on a regular bill in the Senate.
+
+- Banking is the Senate committee that gets FEMA's flood insurance cost recommendations.
+
+Photo: Architect of the Capitol
+The United States Capitol from the west. The Senate meets in the wing on the left, and the House in the wing on the right.
+
+Federal flood law sends FEMA's recommendations on flood insurance costs to the Senate Committee on Banking, Housing, and Urban Affairs . Committee seats for the new Congress are not assigned yet, so Andrews' committees are not known.
+A long-term flood bill needs those Senate votes, a majority in the House and the president's signature. Her first-time buyer tax credit idea needs the same votes, unless it goes in a budget bill, which needs only a simple majority in the Senate.
+CBS News projects that Democrats will control the Senate in January. Before the election, Republicans held 53 seats to 47.
+
+## What have Andrews and Wilson said about insurance?
+Neither winner has a written flood insurance plan that we found. Andrews held a roundtable on homeowners insurance. Wilson's agenda, as reported, calls for state audits of rate increases.
+
+### Annie Andrews
+Democrat. Won the U.S. Senate race; the Associated Press called it on election night.
