+Every county council seat on the ballot went to a Republican, 4 of the 7 without an opponent.
+Who won in the unofficial count, and what each office can change for building here Office Who won What it can change
+Horry County Council Who won Chair Johnny Gardner, re-elected unopposed. District seats, all Republican: 1 Jenna Dukes, 2 Bill Howard, 5 Tyler Servant, 7 Tom Anderson, 8 Michael "Mash" Masciarelli, 11 Al Allen. County results are uncertified. What it can change Impact fees, zoning and the county's flood building rules. State law allows counties and cities to zone .
+Governor Who won Alan Wilson (R), 51.71%. Jermaine Johnson (D) conceded. What it can change Proposes the state's executive budget, where money for I-73 could be asked for.
+S.C. House, 124 seats Who won Republicans 79, Democrats 45, down from 89 Republicans and 35 Democrats. State Senate seats were not on the ballot, except one special race in District 15. What it can change State law, including the law that allows impact fees.
+U.S. Senate Who won Annie Andrews (D), 51.02%. The AP called the race. What it can change Federal law and spending, with the House.
+U.S. House, 7th District Who won Russell Fry (R), 56.75%, re-elected. The AP called the race. What it can change Federal law and spending, for the district that includes Horry County.
+
+Photo: Pollinator
+The Horry County courthouse and county office complex in Conway, the county seat.
+
+CBS News projects that Democrats will control the U.S. Senate in January. Statewide results stay unofficial until the state certifies them. Every race is on our page on what the 2026 election means for Myrtle Beach buyers and sellers .
+
+## What is RIDE IV, the Horry County road tax?
+RIDE IV is a 1% sales tax for road projects in Horry County, approved by voters in November 2024.
+56.78% of Horry voters said yes in 2024
+25 years or $6.35 billion, whichever comes first
+$450 million for I-73 , as WIS reported the list
+
+One item is a local match for I-73 : up to half the cost of building it from SC 22 to the Horry County line. That money can be spent only after SCDOT, the state Department of Transportation, has signed contracts to finish I-73 from Horry County to I-95 . The WIS report on I-73 gives the $450 million figure.
+
+## When will I-73 be built in South Carolina?
+No start date has been announced. Work cannot begin until SCDOT identifies where the money comes from. The South Carolina section is estimated at $1.6 billion, and funding for most of that cost has not been identified.
+I-73 in South Carolina: what has happened When What happened
+November 2024 What happened Horry voters approved RIDE IV. Its I-73 match can be spent only once SCDOT has signed contracts to finish the road to I-95 .
+2024 to 2026 What happened Gov. Henry McMaster left I-73 out of his executive budget in each of the three years.
+February 2026 What happened SCDOT reported the right of way 99% bought between I-95 and US 501 , and 95% between US 501 and the Little Pee Dee River.
+August 2026 What happened President Trump said "they're going to have the funding that's necessary." He named no source for the money.
+
+### What has to happen before building starts
+
+- The money Funding for the rest of the $1.6 billion has to be identified.
+
+- The contracts SCDOT signs the contracts to finish I-73 to I-95 . After that, the RIDE IV match can be spent.
+
+Governor-elect Wilson will propose the next executive budgets. Neither leader's plan names a road project.
+
+## What do the new leaders say about roads, growth and federal money?
+Each plan below is in the leader's own words, from a campaign page or an interview. Each one needs other officials to act before anything changes in Horry County.
+
+### Annie Andrews (D), U.S. Senate
+Roads "Fix and modernize South Carolina's roads, bridges, and highways," and "make sure federal dollars actually reach our communities," from her infrastructure page . Building She would "provide tax cuts to homebuilders to incentivize more construction" and end "chaotic and unilateral tariffs." Her plan names home building materials among the goods tariffs make costlier. Permits She backs cutting "red tape that makes it harder to build new energy infrastructure." Growth A two-year national moratorium on building AI data centers. Who must act Both houses of Congress pass a bill, and the president signs it.
+
+### Alan Wilson (R), governor
+Roads "We need to cut down the time it takes to build a road or a bridge in South Carolina." His priorities say he will "modernize the Department of Transportation" and "streamline project timelines." Building "Make it easier for homes to be built, which lowers the cost of those homes." He tied home costs to waiting on permits . Growth Independent performance audits of state agencies, overseen by the lieutenant governor-elect, Mike Reichenbach. Who must act The governor's office for the audits. A change in state law needs the General Assembly. Home permits are issued by counties and cities.
+
+Rep. Russell Fry (R) was re-elected in the 7th District, which includes Horry County. Any federal road money for I-73 would need votes in both the House and the Senate.
+
+## What are Horry County impact fees on a new home?
+A single-family home of about 1,500 square feet pays $1,615 in total in Horry County impact fees. The fee depends on the kind of building and its size. The county council approved this schedule unanimously in July 2026, as Ordinance 30-2026. Horry County has charged impact fees since 2021.
+$1,615 for a single-family home of about 1,500 square feet
+
+Impact fees are a county power under the state's Development Impact Fee Act . Before charging them on homes, a county must estimate the effect on affordable housing, and the ordinance needs a positive majority of the council. Any change to the fee is a vote of the county council, including the members elected this year.
+On a new home, we ask the builder whether the fee is already in the price, so you compare homes on the same terms. Our guide to buying new construction covers the rest of the process.
+
+## What are the flood building rules for a new home in Horry County?
+In a flood zone, the lowest floor of a new home must be built above the expected flood level. The extra height is called freeboard. In June 2021 the county council advanced an ordinance raising the freeboard for new homes in flood zones from 1 foot to 3 feet. Its final vote was scheduled for July 13, 2021.
+Floor height above the flood level for a new home in a flood zone Left, the old rule of 1 foot. Right, the 3 feet in the ordinance the council advanced in 2021. Our drawing.
+We confirm the current height rule with the county for any lot in a flood zone before you buy, because it changes the cost of the build. What a flood zone means for insurance is on our page on flood insurance after the election and our coastal insurance guide .
+
+## What could happen to new home and land prices in Horry County, short and long term?
+In the next year or two, we expect the pattern of the last five years to continue unless the national market turns. Over five years or more, Horry prices have fallen about 26% (2008 to 2012) and risen to about 2.4 times the low (2012 to 2026).
+What happened in Horry County before Period What happened Source
+2006 to 2010 What happened Single-family permits fell from 6,484 to 1,388, about four in five fewer. Source Census permits
+2008 to 2012 What happened Prices fell about 26%, end of year to end of year. Source FHFA index
+2012 to mid-2026 What happened Prices rose to about 2.4 times the 2012 low. Source FHFA index
+The year after each election, 2016 to 2024 What happened Prices rose every time, by 3% to 22%. Source FHFA index
+2021 to 2025 What happened Permits stayed between 6,356 and 7,269 a year. Source Census permits
+
+### The next one to two years
+Local prices rose in the year after each of the last five elections, whichever party won. The latest yearly change, 0.8%, is inside the index's error of about 2%, so it reads as flat. If the national market holds, we expect permits to stay in the 2021 to 2025 range in the table. If it turns as it did from 2006 to 2010, plan for that slowdown, the first row of the table.
+
+### Five years and longer
+We have no verified past example of what a new interstate did to Horry land prices, so we make no estimate for I-73 .
+Each leader's plan, under the same test Plan Who must act Past Horry example
+Faster road building (Wilson) Who must act The governor's office and the General Assembly Past Horry example None in our data
+Faster home permits (Wilson) Who must act The General Assembly, and county and city councils Past Horry example None in our data
+Homebuilder tax cuts and a tariff change (Andrews) Who must act Both houses of Congress and the president Past Horry example None in our data
+Less red tape for energy projects (Andrews) Who must act Both houses of Congress and the president Past Horry example None in our data
+A pause on AI data centers (Andrews) Who must act Both houses of Congress and the president Past Horry example None in our data
+
+With no past Horry example for any of these plans, we make no estimate for any of them.
