Color" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 21h18M5 21V9.5l5-3V21M10 21V4h9v17M13 8h3M13 12h3M13 16h3M7.5 13h.01M7.5 16.5h.01"/></svg></span><span><strong>Governor</strong><span class="t">Wilson (R)</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 3h8l4 4v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H6a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM14 3v4h4M9 17l6-6"/><circle cx="9.5" cy="11.5" r="1"/><circle cx="14.5" cy="16.5" r="1"/></svg></span><span><strong>State House</strong><span class="t">79 R, 45 D</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg></span><span><strong>Local ballot</strong><span class="t">No questions</span></span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Chief Operating Officer, and <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Abdulla Hijazi</strong>, Chief Marketing Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 4, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">After the election, state House Republicans are 4 votes short of changing the rental tax alone. Most rules for a Myrtle Beach sale are made in Columbia and Conway.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a home bought or sold in Myrtle Beach this winter, the election changed no rule yet. Annie Andrews (D) won the U.S. Senate seat and Alan Wilson (R) the governor's race, and each plan still needs votes first.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the state House, Republicans fell from 89 seats to 79 in the unofficial count. That is 4 short of the 83 votes it takes to change the tax ratio on rentals and second homes.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Tell us the house. We tell you what the election changes for it.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Ask about your house</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#who-won" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Who won</a></li><li><a href="#horry-county" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Horry County</a></li><li><a href="#two-thirds" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">The two-thirds vote</a></li><li><a href="#who-decides" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Who decides what</a></li><li><a href="#andrews-plans" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Andrews' plans</a></li><li><a href="#wilson-plans" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Wilson's plans</a></li><li><a href="#prices" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Prices after elections</a></li><li><a href="#now" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Buyers and sellers now</a></li><li><a href="#2026-election-and-myrtle-beach-real-estate-faq" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">2026 election and Myrtle Beach real estate FAQ</a></li></ul></nav></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="who-won"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Who won">Who won the 2026 election in South Carolina?</h2><style>main#main .el-tabwrap{max-width:760px;margin:1.2rem 0 1.2rem;padding:.7rem 1rem .5rem;border-radius:18px;box-sizing:border-box;background:linear-gradient(180deg,rgba(255,255,255,.78),rgba(255,255,255,.54));border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.75);box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.9),0 18px 40px -26px rgba(28,32,40,.45)}main#main .el-tab{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:1rem;color:var(--navy)}main#main .el-tab caption{text-align:left;font-weight:600;padding:.3rem 0 .6rem;line-height:1.4;font-size:1.0625rem}main#main .el-tab th,main#main .el-tab td{padding:.6rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.12);text-align:left;vertical-align:top;line-height:1.45}main#main .el-tab thead th{font-size:.9rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);letter-spacing:.02em}main#main .el-tab tbody th[scope=row]{font-weight:600;min-width:9rem}main#main .el-tab td .l{display:none}main#main .el-tab td q{font-style:italic;quotes:'\201C' '\201D'}main#main .el-tab tbody tr:last-child>*{border-bottom:0}main#main .el-tab a{color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .el-tabwrap{padding:.6rem .85rem .4rem}main#main .el-tab,main#main .el-tab tbody,main#main .el-tab caption{display:block;width:100%}main#main .el-tab thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0)}main#main .el-tab tr{display:block;padding:.75rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.14)}main#main .el-tab tbody tr:last-child{border-bottom:0}main#main .el-tab th,main#main .el-tab td{display:block;border:0;padding:0 0 .45rem}main#main .el-tab tbody th[scope=row]{font-size:1.0625rem;padding-bottom:.35rem}main#main .el-tab td .l{display:block;font-size:.875rem;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted);letter-spacing:.02em}}main#main svg.el-n{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;max-width:340px;margin:0 auto}main#main svg.el-w{display:none;width:100%;height:auto;max-width:640px}@media (min-width:600px){main#main svg.el-n{display:none}main#main svg.el-w{display:block}}main#main .el-time{list-style:none;margin:1.2rem 0 1.4rem;padding:0;max-width:720px;position:relative}main#main .el-time li{position:relative;margin:0;padding:0 0 1.1rem 2rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6}main#main .el-time li::before{content:'';position:absolute;left:.42rem;top:.5rem;width:.7rem;height:.7rem;border-radius:50%;background:var(--brass);box-shadow:0 0 0 4px rgba(196,120,58,.18)}main#main .el-time li::after{content:'';position:absolute;left:.75rem;top:1.4rem;bottom:0;width:2px;background:rgba(28,32,40,.14)}main#main .el-time li:last-child::after{display:none}main#main .el-time strong{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:1rem}main#main .el-more{display:block;font-weight:400;font-size:.95rem;margin-top:.35rem}main#main .el-other{color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:640px;margin:0 0 1rem}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Annie Andrews, a Democrat, won the U.S. Senate seat over Darline Graham, the Republican appointed to it in July. She is the first woman elected to represent South Carolina in the U.S. Senate.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Alan Wilson, a Republican and the state's attorney general, won the governor's race. Jermaine Johnson, the Democrat, conceded on election night.</p><div class="el-tabwrap"><table class="el-tab"><caption>South Carolina's 2026 results, unofficial count</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Race</th><th scope="col">Winner</th><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col">How it was decided</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">U.S. Senate</th><td><span class="l">Winner</span>Annie Andrews (D)</td><td><span class="l">Result</span>51.0% to 46.8% over Darline Graham (R)</td><td><span class="l">How it was decided</span>The AP called it on election night. CBS News projected it too.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Governor</th><td><span class="l">Winner</span>Alan Wilson (R), with Mike Reichenbach for lieutenant governor</td><td><span class="l">Result</span>51.7% to 47.0% over Jermaine Johnson (D)</td><td><span class="l">How it was decided</span>Johnson conceded on election night.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">U.S. House, 7th District, which includes Horry County</th><td><span class="l">Winner</span>Russell Fry (R)</td><td><span class="l">Result</span>56.8% to 43.2% over John Gregory Vincent (D)</td><td><span class="l">How it was decided</span>The AP called it.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Other statewide offices</th><td><span class="l">Winner</span>Republicans won all six</td><td><span class="l">Result</span>51.1% to 53.1%</td><td><span class="l">How it was decided</span>Unofficial count.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">State House, all 124 seats</th><td><span class="l">Winner</span>79 Republicans, 45 Democrats</td><td><span class="l">Result</span>Before the election: 89 and 35</td><td><span class="l">How it was decided</span>Two races were within 1 percent and go to a recount.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">State Senate, 46 seats</th><td><span class="l">Winner</span>Not on the ballot, except one special race</td><td><span class="l">Result</span>Stays 34 Republicans, 12 Democrats</td><td><span class="l">How it was decided</span>No change.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Ballot questions</th><td><span class="l">Winner</span>None statewide or in Horry County</td><td><span class="l">Result</span>Georgetown County, south of Horry, approved a local sales tax</td><td><span class="l">How it was decided</span>Unofficial count.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">These are unofficial counts until the State Board of Canvassers certifies them. State law requires a recount when a race is within 1 percent. Two House races are that close, so the House split could still move.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In Washington, <a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/news/south-carolina-2026-senate-annie-andrews-darline-graham/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CBS News projects</a> that Democrats will control the U.S. Senate in January. Before the election, Republicans held 53 seats to 47.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="horry-county"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Horry County">How did Horry County vote in 2026?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County voted more Republican than the state. Darline Graham carried the county with about 60 percent, though she lost statewide. Wilson won it with about 64 percent.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No question for Horry County, Myrtle Beach, North Myrtle Beach or Surfside Beach was on the ballot. Nothing on the local ballot changed a tax or a rule for homes.</p><div class="el-tabwrap"><table class="el-tab"><caption>Horry County's local races, uncertified</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Office</th><th scope="col">Winner</th><th scope="col">Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">County Council chairman</th><td><span class="l">Winner</span>Johnny Gardner (R)</td><td><span class="l">Result</span>Unopposed</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">County Council, District 2</th><td><span class="l">Winner</span>Bill Howard (R)</td><td><span class="l">Result</span>74.9% over Kristi Burch (Forward)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">County Council, Districts 5 and 7</th><td><span class="l">Winner</span>Tyler Servant (R) and Tom Anderson (R)</td><td><span class="l">Result</span>84.8% and 69.7%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">County Council, Districts 1, 8 and 11</th><td><span class="l">Winner</span>Jenna Dukes, Michael Masciarelli and Al Allen (all R)</td><td><span class="l">Result</span>Unopposed</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">School board, District 7</th><td><span class="l">Winner</span>Sonya Sasser (R)</td><td><span class="l">Result</span>51.8% to 48.1% over Janet P. Graham (D)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">The 11 state House districts on Horry County's ballot</th><td><span class="l">Winner</span>8 Republicans, 3 Democrats</td><td><span class="l">Result</span>Closest: District 57, Cynthia Ford (D), 51.6%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.6rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/hoa-liens-and-foreclosure/horry-government-justice-center-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/hoa-liens-and-foreclosure/horry-government-justice-center-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/hoa-liens-and-foreclosure/horry-government-justice-center-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="675" alt="The Horry County Government and Justice Center in Conway: brick wings on each side of a white columned entrance, seen across the parking lot" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia. License: CC BY-SA 3.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 3.0">Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia</a></span></div><figcaption>Horry County's government center in Conway. The County Council decides impact fees on new homes and zoning outside the cities.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="two-thirds"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The two-thirds vote">Can the new legislature cut the property tax on Myrtle Beach rentals?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not with Republican votes alone. South Carolina's <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/scconstitution/A10.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">constitution</a> taxes the home you live in on 4 percent of its value, and every other home on 6 percent.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The legislature can change those numbers only with two-thirds of each chamber's members. That is 83 of 124 in the House and 31 of 46 in the Senate.</p><figure style="margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:760px"><svg class="el-n" viewBox="0 0 320 252" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of seats. State House, 124 seats: 79 Republicans and 45 Democrats in the unofficial count; two-thirds is 83 votes, 4 more than the Republicans hold. State Senate, 46 seats: 34 Republicans and 12 Democrats; two-thirds is 31 votes, 3 fewer than the Republicans hold."><text x="2.0" y="18.0" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">State House, 124 seats</text><rect x="2" y="31.5" width="201.3" height="34.5" rx="6" fill="#1c2028"/><rect x="203.3" y="31.5" width="114.7" height="34.5" rx="6" fill="#c9ccd3"/><text x="12.0" y="54.3" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#ffffff">79 R</text><text x="308.0" y="54.3" font-size="15" text-anchor="end" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">45 D</text><line x1="213.5" x2="213.5" y1="25.5" y2="74.0" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="3"/><text x="213.5" y="87.8" font-size="15" text-anchor="end" fill="#1c2028">83 votes is two-thirds</text><text x="213.5" y="106.5" font-size="15" text-anchor="end" font-weight="600" fill="#8a5226">Republicans are 4 short</text><text x="2.0" y="135.0" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">State Senate, 46 seats</text><rect x="2" y="148.5" width="233.6" height="34.5" rx="6" fill="#1c2028"/><rect x="235.6" y="148.5" width="82.4" height="34.5" rx="6" fill="#c9ccd3"/><text x="12.0" y="171.3" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#ffffff">34 R</text><text x="308.0" y="171.3" font-size="15" text-anchor="end" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">12 D</text><line x1="215.0" x2="215.0" y1="142.5" y2="191.0" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="3"/><text x="215.0" y="204.8" font-size="15" text-anchor="end" fill="#1c2028">31 votes is two-thirds</text><text x="215.0" y="223.5" font-size="15" text-anchor="end" font-weight="600" fill="#8a5226">Republicans have 3 more</text></svg><svg class="el-w" viewBox="0 0 640 252" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of seats. State House, 124 seats: 79 Republicans and 45 Democrats in the unofficial count; two-thirds is 83 votes, 4 more than the Republicans hold. State Senate, 46 seats: 34 Republicans and 12 Democrats; two-thirds is 31 votes, 3 fewer than the Republicans hold."><text x="2.0" y="18.0" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">State House, 124 seats</text><rect x="2" y="31.5" width="405.2" height="34.5" rx="6" fill="#1c2028"/><rect x="407.2" y="31.5" width="230.8" height="34.5" rx="6" fill="#c9ccd3"/><text x="12.0" y="54.3" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#ffffff">79 R</text><text x="628.0" y="54.3" font-size="15" text-anchor="end" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">45 D</text><line x1="427.7" x2="427.7" y1="25.5" y2="74.0" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="3"/><text x="427.7" y="87.8" font-size="15" text-anchor="end" fill="#1c2028">83 votes is two-thirds</text><text x="427.7" y="106.5" font-size="15" text-anchor="end" font-weight="600" fill="#8a5226">Republicans are 4 short</text><text x="2.0" y="135.0" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">State Senate, 46 seats</text><rect x="2" y="148.5" width="470.1" height="34.5" rx="6" fill="#1c2028"/><rect x="472.1" y="148.5" width="165.9" height="34.5" rx="6" fill="#c9ccd3"/><text x="12.0" y="171.3" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#ffffff">34 R</text><text x="628.0" y="171.3" font-size="15" text-anchor="end" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">12 D</text><line x1="430.6" x2="430.6" y1="142.5" y2="191.0" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="3"/><text x="430.6" y="204.8" font-size="15" text-anchor="end" fill="#1c2028">31 votes is two-thirds</text><text x="430.6" y="223.5" font-size="15" text-anchor="end" font-weight="600" fill="#8a5226">Republicans have 3 more</text></svg><figcaption style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.6;margin-top:.6rem">Seats after the 2026 election. R is Republicans and D is Democrats. The brass line is the two-thirds vote it takes to change the tax ratio on rentals and second homes. House seats are the unofficial count.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Republicans hold 34 Senate seats, more than 31. In the House they won 79, so even if every Republican votes yes, a change needs at least 4 Democratic votes. A new property tax exemption needs the same two-thirds.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The other path is a constitutional amendment. It needs two-thirds of each chamber, then a majority of voters in November 2028, then a second vote by the next legislature.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our page on <a href="/invest/rental-property-tax-cut/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Wilson's rental tax cut</a> works through what a lower ratio would save on a rental here.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="who-decides"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Who decides what">Who decides what about a Myrtle Beach home?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Congress decides flood insurance and federal taxes. The legislature in Columbia decides the property tax ratios. The county and city councils decide zoning and fees.</p><div class="el-tabwrap"><table class="el-tab"><caption>Who decides what about a home on the Grand Strand</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Who</th><th scope="col">Who holds it now</th><th scope="col">What it decides for a home here</th><th scope="col">More</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Congress, in Washington</th><td><span class="l">Who holds it now</span>Annie Andrews joins the Senate in January. Russell Fry represents Horry County in the House.</td><td><span class="l">What it decides for a home here</span>Federal flood insurance. Federal tax credits for buyers. The tax break on a home sale gain. Limits on large investors.</td><td><span class="l">More</span><a href="/buyers/flood-insurance-after-the-election/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Flood insurance</a>, <a href="/buyers/first-time-buyer-tax-credit/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">buyer tax credit</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">The governor and the legislature, in Columbia</th><td><span class="l">Who holds it now</span>Governor-elect Alan Wilson. A House of 79 Republicans and 45 Democrats, and a Senate of 34 and 12.</td><td><span class="l">What it decides for a home here</span>The 4 and 6 percent tax ratios. The school tax exemption on a home you live in. HOA law. The state tax on short-stay rentals. Whether cities may limit short-term rentals.</td><td><span class="l">More</span><a href="/invest/rental-property-tax-cut/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Rental tax cut</a>, <a href="/buyers/act-388-changes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Act 388, the 2006 school tax law</a>, <a href="/invest/short-term-rental-rules-statehouse/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">short-term rentals</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Horry County Council, in Conway</th><td><span class="l">Who holds it now</span>Chairman Johnny Gardner and the district members.</td><td><span class="l">What it decides for a home here</span>Impact fees on new homes. Zoning outside the cities.</td><td><span class="l">More</span><a href="/invest/horry-county-growth-roads-permits/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Growth, roads and permits</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">City and town councils</th><td><span class="l">Who holds it now</span>Myrtle Beach, North Myrtle Beach and each town have their own council.</td><td><span class="l">What it decides for a home here</span>Zoning inside the city, including where short-term rentals are allowed. Business licenses for rentals.</td><td><span class="l">More</span><a href="/invest/short-term-rental-rules-statehouse/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Short-term rentals</a>, <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental rules</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We found no statement on short-term rentals from either winner. No state law stops a city from limiting them today.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Ask what the election changes for your house</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us the address. We tell you its flood zone, its tax ratio and what a pending vote could change for it.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Ask about your house</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="andrews-plans"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Andrews' plans">What are Annie Andrews' policies on housing and taxes?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Andrews proposes nine things that touch homes or what owners pay, from her <a href="https://drannieandrews.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Web-file.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">affordability plan</a> and <a href="https://drannieandrews.com/platform/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">platform</a>. Each is a proposal. A federal law needs a House majority, the Senate and the president.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the Senate, most bills need 60 votes to end debate. A budget bill under the reconciliation process passes with a simple majority.</p><div class="el-tabwrap"><table class="el-tab"><caption>Annie Andrews' plans that touch a home here</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Plan</th><th scope="col">What the campaign said</th><th scope="col">What it would touch here</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">A $50,000 tax credit for first-time buyers<span class="el-more">More: <a href="/buyers/first-time-buyer-tax-credit/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">What the last credit did</a></span></th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span><q>a $50,000 tax cut to help first-time homebuyers</q></td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>A first-time buyer's down payment, and how many buyers bid on starter homes. She has no bill of her own, and the closest bill has had no vote.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Cut the limit on large investors from 350 homes to 50</th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span><q>We should cut that threshold down several times to 50 homes.</q></td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>Big rental companies buying houses here. The 350-home limit, which became law in July, starts Jan. 7, 2027.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Bar Wall Street investors from Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac loans</th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span><q>ban these Wall Street interests from using Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac mortgages to buy up housing</q></td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>Large investors buying homes here with those loans.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Tax cuts for home builders and more workforce housing<span class="el-more">More: <a href="/invest/horry-county-growth-roads-permits/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Building in Horry County</a></span></th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span><q>provide tax cuts to homebuilders to incentivize more construction</q></td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>How many new homes go up. Horry County issued permits for 6,418 new single-family homes in 2025.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">End tariffs the president imposes alone</th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span><q>end chaotic and unilateral tariffs</q>, naming home building materials</td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>The cost of building a new home here.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A two-year national pause on AI data centers</th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span>A two-year national moratorium on AI data centers, and no data center costs passed on to ratepayers</td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>Data center building, and power bills.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Roads, bridges and resilient infrastructure<span class="el-more">More: <a href="/invest/horry-county-growth-roads-permits/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Roads and I-73</a></span></th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span><q>Fix and modernize South Carolina's roads, bridges, and highways</q></td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>Federal road money for Horry County. She named no project and no I-73 plan.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Homeowners insurance<span class="el-more">More: <a href="/buyers/flood-insurance-after-the-election/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Flood insurance after the election</a></span></th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span>She held a roundtable in Beaufort on rising homeowners insurance. She has no written flood insurance plan.</td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>Insurance on coastal homes. The federal flood program's deadline comes before she takes office.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Three income tax brackets, and no tax rise under $500,000</th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span>Brackets of 10%, 20% and 35%. Families earning over $400,000 would pay 35% on all their income.</td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>Income tax. The tax break on a home sale gain is not in her plan.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.6rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-e/election-2026/us-capitol-west-front-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-e/election-2026/us-capitol-west-front-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-e/election-2026/us-capitol-west-front-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="622" alt="The west front of the U.S. Capitol, with its white dome between the Senate and House wings, behind a wide green lawn" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:PD-USGov-Congress-AOC" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: Architect of the Capitol. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: Architect of the Capitol</a></span></div><figcaption>The U.S. Capitol in Washington, where Congress votes on federal flood insurance and on any tax credit for buyers.</figcaption></figure><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Where a buyer tax credit was tried before</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The federal credit of 2008 to 2010 paid up to $8,000. A study in the Journal of Finance found it raised home sales by about 490,000, near 10 percent. It raised prices about 1 percent by the study's measure. Much of the extra demand came from buyers who would have bought several years later. The authors judged that its benefit in steadying the market likely exceeded its direct boost to buying.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Here it did not stop a fall. The Myrtle Beach-area price index dropped about 10 percent in 2009 and kept falling until 2012.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What it could mean for a $50,000 credit</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If a $50,000 credit passed, the same pattern points to more sales and higher prices in the first years. It is about six times the old credit, and no study we found measured a credit that size. How much prices would rise, then or later, is unknown. Our page on the <a href="/buyers/first-time-buyer-tax-credit/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">first-time buyer tax credit</a> shows what the last one did.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Her other plans, with no direct tie to a home sale</h3><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Health care.</strong> Reverse federal Medicaid cuts, expand Medicaid in South Carolina, and restore expired marketplace premium help. Limit insurer profits per enrollee, let regulators reject large premium increases, replace prior authorization, and add a public option.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Drug prices.</strong> Imports from Canada, the $35 insulin cap for every insured patient, patent reform, rules for drug middlemen, and more drug prices that Medicare negotiates.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Families and work.</strong> A child care worker apprenticeship, tax credits for child care businesses, a permanent, larger child tax credit, higher wages and paid family leave.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Taxes and spending.</strong> End tax breaks for the highest earners, the carried interest break and breaks for moving jobs overseas. Keep no tax on tips and Social Security for good. Restore free IRS filing. Vote against debt ceiling increases until a balanced budget passes.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Energy.</strong> Suspend the federal gas tax, restore clean energy tax cuts, and cut red tape for new energy projects.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Congress.</strong> Term limits, and two Senate terms for herself. A balanced budget amendment, a stock trading ban, no pay in shutdowns, no congressional pensions, no earmarks, and stronger fraud checks.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Other.</strong> Universal background checks and safe gun storage, a path to citizenship with border security, a digital bill of rights, phones out of schools, Roe v. Wade protections, a ban on corporate PAC and dark money, and a vote of Congress before military force.</li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="wilson-plans"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Wilson's plans">What are Alan Wilson's plans for property tax and housing?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Wilson proposes nine things that touch homes or what owners pay. They come from his <a href="https://wilsonforsc.com/priorities/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">priorities</a>, his agenda as <a href="https://www.foxcarolina.com/2026/10/15/republican-nominee-sc-governor-alan-wilson-announces-affordability-agenda/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fox Carolina reported it</a>, and an <a href="https://www.wistv.com/2026/10/28/full-interview-alan-wilson-shares-priorities-governor-ahead-election-day/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">interview with WIS</a>. Each is a proposal. A state law needs a majority of both chambers and his signature.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A change to the 4 and 6 percent tax ratios needs two-thirds of each chamber.</p><div class="el-tabwrap"><table class="el-tab"><caption>Alan Wilson's plans that touch a home here</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Plan</th><th scope="col">What the campaign said</th><th scope="col">What it would touch here</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Lower the higher tax on rentals (Act 388)<span class="el-more">More: <a href="/invest/rental-property-tax-cut/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">What it would mean for investors</a></span></th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span><q>obviously looking at Act 388 and reforming how we assess property taxes on those types of properties</q></td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>The yearly tax bill on every rental and second home here, taxed at 6 percent. It needs 83 House and 31 Senate votes, and he has published no bill.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Enroll seniors in the homestead exemption automatically</th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span>Auto-enrolling seniors for the homestead exemption, as reported</td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>The tax bill on a senior's own home.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A larger homestead exemption for disabled veterans</th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span>Expanding the homestead exemption for disabled veterans, as reported</td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>The tax bill on a disabled veteran's own home.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">End the car tax for seniors and veterans</th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span><q>He will work to eliminate the car property tax for seniors and veterans</q></td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>The yearly tax on their cars.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Lower personal property taxes on businesses</th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span>Lowering personal property taxes for commercial businesses, as reported</td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>Taxes on the equipment of local businesses.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">End the state income tax and overhaul property taxes</th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span><q>I still want to eliminate the income tax.</q> He calls it a long-term goal. Overhauling property taxes is in his agenda, as reported.</td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>Income tax, and how property is taxed here.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Public audits of insurance rate increases<span class="el-more">More: <a href="/buyers/flood-insurance-after-the-election/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Insurance after the election</a></span></th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span>Regular, public audits of rate increases by the Department of Insurance, which already has audit power, as reported</td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>Homeowners insurance rates. Flood insurance is federal, so it is not part of this plan.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Faster permits for new homes<span class="el-more">More: <a href="/invest/horry-county-growth-roads-permits/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Permits in Horry County</a></span></th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span><q>make it easier for homes to be built, which lowers the cost of those homes</q></td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>Waits for building permits on new homes.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Faster roads, and a modernized Department of Transportation<span class="el-more">More: <a href="/invest/horry-county-growth-roads-permits/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Roads and I-73</a></span></th><td><span class="l">What the campaign said</span><q>we need to cut down the time it takes to build a road or a bridge</q></td><td><span class="l">What it would touch here</span>Road projects such as I-73. Funding for most of its $1.6 billion cost has not been identified.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Where a property tax shift was tried before</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Act 388, a state tax law passed in 2006, removed school operating taxes from homes people live in. It added a 1 percent sales tax to make up the money.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At 4 percent, the yearly bill on a rental inside Myrtle Beach would fall by about a third. In Chapter3's files, property tax stops more rental deals here than insurance does.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Yearly property tax on a $350,000 home inside Myrtle Beach"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Yearly property tax on a $350,000 home inside Myrtle Beach</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">The home, how it is taxed</span><span role="columnheader">Tax a year</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">As a rental or second home today</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">6 percent ratio</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.764" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about $5,350</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">If rentals moved to 4 percent</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">still paying school operating tax</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.509" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about $3,560</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">As the home you live in</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">4 percent, no school operating tax</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.291" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about $2,040</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Horry County's 2026 tax levy for county, school and city purposes. Before credits, fees and any special district.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A 2020 Lincoln Institute study found that the state's system has shifted the property tax burden from homes people live in to rentals, second homes and business property. The South Carolina Chamber Foundation and South Carolina Realtors chose its case-study counties.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The study counts a group it calls other residential, typically rentals taxed at 6 percent. In Horry County in 2018, that group was 36.4 percent of the appraised value of the property the county assessor values. It was 47.2 percent of the value the tax is figured on. Act 388 has defenders too, such as nohometax.org.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No plan names who would make up the lost tax. No study we found shows whether the saving would reach tenants or prices. Our page on <a href="/buyers/act-388-changes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">what Act 388 changes would mean</a> looks at both sides.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">His other plans, with no direct tie to a home sale</h3><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Gas.</strong> Let the governor pause the state gas tax for up to 120 days, as reported.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Schools.</strong> Fully fund public schools and expand school choice.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Health.</strong> Rural clinics and telemedicine.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Benefits.</strong> Deny state benefits to people here illegally.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>State agencies.</strong> Independent performance audits, led by the lieutenant governor.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Utilities.</strong> Build the energy grid and add competition, as reported.</li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="prices"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Prices after elections">Will Myrtle Beach home prices go down after the election?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Myrtle Beach-area home prices have not fallen after any of the last five elections. In the year after each one, from 2016 to 2024, prices rose by about 3 to 22 percent.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Change in Myrtle Beach-area home prices in the year after each election"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Change in Myrtle Beach-area home prices in the year after each election</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Year, kind of election</span><span role="columnheader">Change in prices</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Year after the 2016 election</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">presidential election</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.225" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">+5.6%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Year after the 2018 election</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">midterm election</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.2" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">+5.0%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Year after the 2020 election</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">presidential election</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.864" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">+21.6%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Year after the 2022 election</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">midterm election</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.34" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">+8.5%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Year after the 2024 election</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">presidential election</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.119" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">+3.0%</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Latest year, to mid-2026</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">for comparison</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.033" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">+0.8%</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Federal Housing Finance Agency house price index for the Myrtle Beach, Conway and North Myrtle Beach metro area, from the last quarter of each election year to the same quarter a year later. FHFA gives recent index values a standard error of about 2 percent. A change of a few percent, such as +3.0% after 2024 or +0.8% in the latest year, may be within that error.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The largest rise came after 2020 and the smallest after 2024. Prices rose after presidential and midterm elections alike. After the 2008 election, during the housing slump, the index fell about 10 percent in a year.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the latest year, to mid-2026, prices here rose about 0.8 percent. The record gives no basis for a forecast after this election. Our <a href="/sell/home-value/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">home value page</a> shows what homes like yours sold for this year.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="now"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Buyers and sellers now">What changes for Myrtle Beach buyers and sellers now?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a sale this winter, two dates matter: the flood insurance deadline and the new limit on large investors.</p><ol class="el-time"><li><strong>After the count</strong>The State Board of Canvassers certifies the results. House Districts 27 and 89, each within 1 percent, get a recount.</li><li><strong>Dec. 11, 2026</strong>The federal flood insurance program can no longer write new policies unless Congress extends it. This vote comes before Andrews takes office.</li><li><strong>Jan. 3, 2027</strong>Andrews' Senate term begins.</li><li><strong>Jan. 7, 2027</strong>A company that controls 350 or more single-family homes may not buy more, with some exceptions.</li><li><strong>November 2028</strong>The next general election. It is the first time voters could approve a change to the tax ratios in the constitution.</li></ol><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">If you are buying</h3><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Flood insurance.</strong> If Congress lets the federal program lapse on Dec. 11, no new federal flood policies can be written. A loan on a house in FEMA's high-risk zone can then wait for the program to return or for a private policy. In June 2010, by one estimate, over 1,400 closings a day were canceled or delayed across the country. Our agents check a house's flood zone before you make an offer. <a href="/buyers/flood-insurance-after-the-election/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Flood insurance after the election</a>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>No first-time buyer credit today.</strong> The $50,000 credit Andrews backs has had no vote in Congress. Plan a purchase this winter without it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>A rental or second home still pays the 6 percent ratio.</strong> Price it on today's tax bill. A cut needs a two-thirds vote, and Wilson has published no bill.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Large investors.</strong> The limit on companies with 350 or more homes does not touch a family buying one rental.</li></ul><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">If you are selling</h3><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Prices are about flat.</strong> They rose about 0.8 percent in the latest year. We price your home from this year's sales of homes like it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>A buyer's closing can slip.</strong> If your house is in the high-risk flood zone and your buyer needs a loan, a lapse on Dec. 11 can delay the closing. A private flood policy can count if it meets federal rules, and in the 2025 lapse some lenders waived the requirement.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The tax on your gain does not change.</strong> Up to $250,000 of gain on a home you lived in stays out of federal income tax. For certain married couples filing jointly, it is $500,000. Neither winner's plan changes it.</li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="2026-election-and-myrtle-beach-real-estate-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">2026 election and Myrtle Beach real estate FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who won the Senate race in South Carolina in 2026?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Annie Andrews, a Democrat, won with about 51 percent of the unofficial count over Darline Graham, a Republican. The AP called the race on election night, and her term begins in January.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who won the governor's race, and is it official?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Alan Wilson, a Republican, won with about 52 percent, and Jermaine Johnson conceded on election night. Every result is unofficial until the State Board of Canvassers certifies it.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Will Myrtle Beach home prices go down after the election?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the year after each election from 2016 to 2024, the Myrtle Beach-area price index rose, by about 3 to 22 percent. In the latest year prices were about flat, up 0.8 percent.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Did anything on the Horry County ballot affect real estate?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. There was no county, city or statewide ballot question. The ballot held only races for office, from Congress and governor to County Council and school board.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can Alan Wilson cut the property tax on rentals by himself?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. Changing the 6 percent ratio needs two-thirds of each chamber, 83 House votes and 31 Senate votes. Republicans won 79 House seats in the unofficial count, so a change needs Democratic votes too.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://scvotes.gov/2026-general-election-results/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Election Commission results</a>, <a href="https://www.wmbfnews.com/2026/11/04/election-results-whos-winning-grand-strand-pee-dee/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WMBF Horry results</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/scconstitution/A10.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Constitution Art. X</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/scconstitution/A16.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Art. XVI</a>, <a href="https://drannieandrews.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Web-file.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Andrews plan</a>, <a href="https://www.foxcarolina.com/2026/10/15/republican-nominee-sc-governor-alan-wilson-announces-affordability-agenda/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wilson agenda (Fox Carolina)</a>, <a href="https://www.wistv.com/2026/10/28/full-interview-alan-wilson-shares-priorities-governor-ahead-election-day/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wilson interview (WIS)</a>, <a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/data/hpi" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FHFA house price index</a>, <a href="https://www.congress.gov/crs_external_products/IN/HTML/IN10835.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CRS on flood insurance</a>, <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-119publ101/html/PLAW-119publ101.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public Law 119-101</a>. Results are the unofficial count, not certified. Educational only, not legal or tax advice.</p><style>.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Horry County's government center in Conway. The County Council decides impact fees on new homes and zoning outside the cities. Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horry_County_Courthouse_and_county_office_complex,_Conway,_South_Carolina_(18_November_2006).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>. Chapter3 Realty cropped and resized the photo.</div><div role="listitem">The U.S. Capitol in Washington, where Congress votes on federal flood insurance and on any tax credit for buyers. Photo: Architect of the Capitol, <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:PD-USGov-Congress-AOC" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:United_States_Capitol_-_west_front.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>. Chapter3 Realty resized the photo and cut 1x1, 4x3 and 16x9 center crops.</div><div role="listitem">Chapter3 Realty resized these photos and cropped some of them. Each photo keeps the license listed with it.</div></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Ask what the election changes for your house.</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">Send us a house you are buying or selling. An agent tells you what is decided now and what is still a proposal.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Ask about your house</a></div></section></main>
